      "latency_ms": 24004.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1291,
      "output": "**Water** is a chemical substance that is essential for life on Earth. Chemically, it is defined by the formula **H₂O**, which means it is made up of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, how it is structured, and why it is unique:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **Composition:** As mentioned, it consists of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n*   **Polarity:** The oxygen atom is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than the hydrogen atoms. This causes the electrons to spend more time near the oxygen, creating a slight negative charge on one side of the molecule and a slight positive charge on the other.\n*   **The Result:** This polarity makes water a **polar molecule**. It acts like a magnet for other polar substances, which is why water is such a good solvent (it can dissolve many things).\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When water freezes, the molecules lock into a rigid, crystalline structure. This is why ice floats on liquid water (unlike most other substances).\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking, washing, and swimming. It is the only state that can cover the surface of the Earth in large amounts.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When water is heated, it turns into a gas. In the atmosphere, this is called water vapor.\n\n### 3. Key Properties\nWater has several unique properties that make it vital for life:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to transport nutrients and minerals inside living organisms and move them through the environment.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick tightly to one another, creating a \"skin\" on the surface. This allows small insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots against gravity.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and release a lot of heat before it gets cold. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth (oceans) and keeps the human body from overheating.\n\n### 4. Occurrence\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It is found in:\n*   Oceans and seas (salt water)\n*   Rivers, lakes, and glaciers (fresh water)\n*   Underground aquifers\n*   The atmosphere (as vapor and clouds)\n*   Living organisms (humans are about 60% water)"
